      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine how many books Melvin has left after repacking them into boxes containing 40 books each.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of books:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of books} = 1335 \\text{ boxes} \\times 39 \\text{ books/box} = 52065 \\text{ books}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how many full boxes of 40 books each can be packed, and how many books are left over.\n\nThe number of full boxes that can be packed is given by the floor division of the total number of books by 40:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of full 40-book boxes} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{52065}{40} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nCalculate this value:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{52065}{40} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1301.625 \\right\\rfloor = 1301\n\\]\n\nThis means Melvin can pack 1301 boxes with 40 books each. \n\nTo find out how many books are leftover, we calculate the remainder when 52065 books are divided by 40. \n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of books leftover} = 52065 \\bmod 40\n\\]\n\nTo compute this, we use Python's modulo operator (%) to find the remainder:\n```python\ntotal_books = 52065\nbooks_per_box = 40\n\n# calculate the number of full boxes and the leftover books\nfull_boxes = total_books // books_per_box  # division to get the number of full boxes\nleftover_books = total_books % books_per_box  # modulo operation to find remainder\n\nprint(leftover_books)\n```\n```output\n15\n```\nAfter repacking 1301 boxes with 40 books each, Melvin is left with 15 books that cannot be packed into full boxes of 40 books.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
