      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many books Melvin has left over after repacking them into boxes with 40 books each, we start by calculating the total number of books in the warehouse. Since there are 1335 boxes and each box contains 39 books, the total number of books is:\n\n\\[\n1335 \\times 39\n\\]\n\nTo compute \\( 1335 \\times 39 \\), we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n1335 \\\\\n\\times  \\, \\, 39 \\\\\n\\hline\n12065 \\quad \\text{(first part)} \\\\\n4005 \\quad \\text{(second part)}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nSumming these results gives:\n\n\\[\n12065 + 4005 = 16070\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 16070 books in total. Next, we need to determine how many complete boxes of 40 books can be packed from 16070 books. This is done by dividing 16070 by 40:\n\n\\[\n16070 \\div 40 = 401.75\n\\]\n\nSince Melvin can only use whole boxes, we round down to the nearest whole number. Therefore, he can pack 401 boxes with 40 books each. To find out how many books are left over, we calculate the remainder when 16070 is divided by 40:\n\n\\[\n16070 \\mod 40\n\\]\n\nUsing long division, we find:\n\n\\[\n16070 \\div 40 = 401 \\ \\text{with a remainder of} \\ 30\n\\]\n\nHence, the number of books left over after packing as many boxes as possible is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{30}\n\\]",
